Z||000007||Tigger||10-26-2001||08:39 PM||||Sure can!<P>Drop springs are the most popular and economical method, well besides cutting and heating.  Common drops are 1" to 1-1/2".<P>Drop spindles raise the spindle height to give the drop and maintain the stock geometry.  Adjustable coil overs are nice for the rear and allow more clearance for wider tires.  Both of these are more expensive but are great if you want to squeeze the most handling out of your Fiero.<P>I've got 2" Street Dreams Drop spindles up front (see Parts Vendor list at the bottom of this page) and 1-1/2" Suspension Technique rear springs from JCWhitney (I've heard they are currently out of stock).<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue4.gif">      <BR>T-T-F-N, Ta Ta For Now!<BR><A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/tatooine/spielberg/241/" TARGET=_blank>Tigger's Fiero Page</A><p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-26-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000008||artherd||10-27-2001||03:19 AM||||I can't belive I'm saying this...<P>But I've actually had a good expierence with cutting the front springs on my Formula...<P>There, I said it.  Sheesh, I still can't belive it.  <P>My car is an 88 model, it came with 'ARD' rated springs, I wouldn't even attempt this with a previous year car, and certinly not with an 88 with harder springs either.<P>All that said, I cut *exactly* ONE coil off the lower part of each spring...<P>Lowered the car approximately 1" which was just perfect.   With my 17" wheels and 215/45/17 front tyres, the gap at static height is just perfect.<P>And here's the thing.  It didn't **** up the ride.    Spring rate was increased, but only slightly.  Infact, it is desireable to increase spring rate slightly when lowering suspension travel range, because you must ensure that just as the wheels are bottoming out, that you have the same total tension as before.  IE, so your wheels will still have the same resistance to bottoming out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Generally, when you just hack off parts of OE springs, you get entirely too much increase in spring rate, however with ARD springs cut exactly one coil, on an 88 Formula (stock 2.8 v6, battery relocated to the front.)<P>The car now sits about 1/2" lower in front than it does in the rear.  Driving dynamics are improved *all* around, it's smoother, gripier, less jaring, and turns like a deamon with it's tail dunked in ice water.<P>Visually perfect too.<P>PS: the real proper way to do all this is with droped spindles, which retain the geometry of the suspension, and ajustable coilover front suspension.  (both ajustable springs and ride height thereof, AND ajustable bound/rebound shocks.  Eg. Held Motorsports setup.  That is where I am headed, but this should last me a year or so untill then...<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||Oreif||10-27-2001||10:28 AM||||Mine is lowered 1.5" using Eibach springs.<BR>I also am using 225/50R15 tires all the way around.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fcol_01_1.jpg">  <P><BR>------------------<BR>             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">           <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 10-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||10-27-2001||11:19 AM||||Ben,<P>That's encouraging.<BR>When I do my V-6 swap into the 88 coupe, I think I'll try cutting down the front springs. Since they are(?) just a bit softer than the Formula, it should be interesting. <BR>Gonna keep the stock length Formula springs in the rear. (Transplanting the whole cradle.)<BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Some mornings, it just isn't worth chewing through the restraints.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Kasdan||10-27-2001||03:40 PM||||mines low enough for me...im using 205/50R15's Yokohamas.<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan, 1986 GT<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero21.jpg">            <BR>Aftermarket Power Steering, Rebuilt motor with &gt;100 miles!<BR>Aim=FieroDriver1986||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||10-27-2001||03:56 PM||||I highly recommend you do it the right way, either dropped spindles or lowering springs. I do like the looks better with an inch or two lower but i do lots of backroad driving, and since the handling is fine, I like having the extra height and wheel travel. I think the factory ride height is 7" measured from ground up to rocker panel bottom just behind front wheel opening and just ahead of rear wheel. Remember there are parts of the car underneath that are lower than rocker panels too. You dont want to have your oil pan just an inch or two from the road, or your brake lines...............etc.<P>Oh yea Orief, I luv your hood.<P>------------------<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885">  <p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 10-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||TRiAD||10-27-2001||04:17 PM||||I second that, no offense Art, but cut springs are for those rusty domestic crap sitting in front of a double wide with 13" of grass growing up through the hood...<P>Since we have real options out there, and they aren't expensive, buy some springs man!<P>Sorry, my $.02<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000014||qwikgta||10-28-2001||12:22 AM||||I cut a coil and 1/2 off the front and a coil off the rear of my 88 coupe and it sits perfect now, just the right hight with my 16" GTZ rims.  And just to set the record straight, for the 88's its not cheap to do, just try to find dropped spindles for under $500.  Yea, coil over can be done for around $300 but you still have to do the front.<P>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000015||artherd||10-28-2001||01:04 AM||||If you had asked me a week ago, I would have totally agreed  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Come ride in my car, it will warp your opinion on cutting springs (at least for one coil off ARD springs on an 88 car...)<P>The spring rate intrease is just perfect, and uncanny... (the $300 saved I can put into stupid blue headlights  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I wouldn't even try this with a pre-88 car however.  The suspension geometry is so different, and with the shock not concentrically mounted with the spring... messy.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>I second that, no offense Art, but cut springs are for those rusty domestic crap sitting in front of a double wide with 13" of grass growing up through the hood...<P>Since we have real options out there, and they aren't expensive, buy some springs man!<P>Sorry, my $.02<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
